// TEST: Row Map Returning A Literal Of Whole Rows (rejects)
// REJECT-AT: codegen
// ERROR: BL7001
// ERROR-CONTAINS: code generation has no rule for IRArrayLit
// WARN-CODEGEN: unsupported IR node: IRArrayLit
// ============================================================================
// THE OTHER HALF of the array-literal row map, and the pin on how an
// unhandled IR node is REPORTED.
//
// 151-153 cover a literal of SCALARS, whose leaves tile the destination row.
// A literal whose elements are themselves whole ARRAYS (`[r0, r1]`, the
// `rowTypedElemArr` branch of inferArrayLitType) is a different construction:
// it does not flatten to a fixed cell count at the write site, so codegen has
// no rule for it and says so.
//
// What is pinned here is the DELIVERY. A node reaching a codegen catch-all
// means the back end grew no arm for that shape -- a gap in the compiler, not
// a statement about this program -- and it used to surface as g++ complaining
// that `BLADE_CODEGEN_ERROR_UNSUPPORTED_IR_NODE_IRARRAYLIT` was not declared:
// no code, no file, no line. It is now a coded, spanned Blade diagnostic
// (BL7001, backend limit), refused by the driver before the C++ compiler is
// invoked at all. The `#error` guard is still emitted, which is what this
// codegen-stage probe's verdict reads.
//
// Deliberate codegen refusals -- the ones carrying a sentence that says what
// to write instead -- are unaffected and keep their own wording.
// ============================================================================
let ts = (method_for(range<Idx<6>>) <@> lambda(i) -> 1.0 * i) |> compute
let r0 = (method_for(range<Idx<3>>) <@> lambda(k) -> 1.0 * k) |> compute
let r1 = (method_for(range<Idx<3>>) <@> lambda(k) -> 2.0 * k) |> compute
let g = (ts <@> lambda(t) -> [r0, r1]) |> compute
